Title: TERMINOLOGY.md — a standalone glossary for the specification chain
Created: 2026-08-29
Author: claude
Blocked-by: 0036

--- log ---
2026-08-29T00:00Z claude created

--- body ---
## Context
The specification chain has no terminology section. Terms of art are used
without definition, or defined once in passing and then relied on far from
that point: "the ledger", "graft", "kill 9", "corpus-critic M4", "the
wall", "entry segmenter", "stage keys", "coverage", "metadata-only",
"prefix" in R26's sense. A reader meeting one of these in a ticket has
nowhere to look it up.

0036 required that "every term of art [be] defined where it first appears",
and its rewrite delivered roughly twenty jargon glosses. That is the right
first move and it is not sufficient: a gloss at first appearance serves a
reader going top to bottom, and nobody reads a specification that way twice.
Tickets, reviews and forge threads enter the documents in the middle.

The author's ruling (2026-08-29): a standalone document, not a section
inside one of the three.

Standalone also settles an ownership question the chain would otherwise
have. A term used by REQUIREMENTS.md, CONSTRAINTS.md and DESIGN.md alike
belongs to none of them; putting the glossary in any one would either
duplicate definitions or make two documents depend on a third's internals.
This is the one-statement-per-fact rule applied to vocabulary.

## Relevant files
- `TERMINOLOGY.md` — new, at the repository root
- `REQUIREMENTS.md`, `CONSTRAINTS.md`, `DESIGN.md` — inline glosses that
  become redundant may collapse to the term alone; a first-use gloss that
  carries argument stays
- `CLAUDE.md`, `README.md` — document-set list and table
- `tickets/AGENTS.md` — no change expected

## Actions
1. Harvest the vocabulary. Sweep the chain plus the open tickets for terms
   used as though defined. Include Zotero-side vocabulary the project
   inherits (item, attachment, version cursor, fulltext cache) — a newcomer
   does not know which words are ours and which are Zotero's, and that
   distinction is worth marking per entry.
2. Write each entry as: the term, one-sentence definition, and where it is
   authoritative. Definitions POINT at the owning document for anything
   numeric; the glossary owns no thresholds.
3. Mark deprecated and panel-era terms explicitly as historical, with the
   git coordinate, so a reader meeting one in an old ticket is not left
   guessing whether it is live.
4. Register the document in CLAUDE.md and README.md.

## Test
An adherence check that fails when a term listed in TERMINOLOGY.md carries
a numeric threshold in its definition — the glossary must stay a pointer,
not a second home for design numbers. Red first against a seeded entry.

## Verification
- [ ] Every term in the glossary resolves to an owning document
- [ ] No design number is restated in a definition
- [ ] A reader can enter DESIGN.md at a random section and look up every
      unfamiliar term

## Invariants
- The glossary defines; it never decides. A definition that would settle an
  open design question goes to DECISIONS.md first.
- One statement per fact: no threshold, budget or rule is duplicated here.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] `TERMINOLOGY.md` exists, covering the chain's vocabulary and the
      inherited Zotero vocabulary, each entry marked ours or Zotero's
- [ ] Historical and panel-era terms marked as such with a git coordinate
- [ ] Registered in CLAUDE.md and README.md
- [ ] The no-numbers guard is in place and `make check` passes
